cted from promotion and  advancement so a lectureship that is  permanent has good job security  generally speaking um  but in uh but promotion is not  guaranteed you have to apply for  promotion  um sort of going through a round a  series of  um of  applications uh that is different in  nature than the tenure system in the u.s  so i got my position in  uh 2015 and since then i've sat on  several search committees so this is  really informed for me by by time on  both sides of that particular table  one thing to know about applications is  that the timeline  for  applying for and receiving an academic  job in the uk works very differently  often there's a much shorter period  between jobs being announced and the  interviews being held it's not  necessarily conforming to the academic  calendar that we're familiar with  another difference that i found really  fascinating was the fact that in the uk  the norm is that you have all job talks  on the same day  and i was actually able to attend all  the other job talks for the same  position that we were all applying for  um in the us it's quite common to be  expected to go out to dinner with the  people who are interviewing you in the  uk we were told as job applicants and  candidates for the same job to go and  have dinner together without anyone from  the department along so a very different  approach i think to sociability  um in that context of job applications  the job talk was also very short it was  15 minutes  and it was not as important as the  formal interview so the whole process  was held over two days and the formal  interview on day two was  a much more kind of formal panel  along with that there are significant  legal restrictions on what can be asked  in those formal interviews in front of a  panel  most of the questions in that interview  were  i would say pretty similar to what you  might get in  interviews for a north american job in  academia  one crucial difference though i think is  that demonstrating