ingapore it's very important not just  for uh job application but also for  ground application as well  um i can go next um  so um  at dku i i don't think um starting some  something outside of asia is not is a  problem because we sometimes  intentionally hire um faculty who  who teaches  um  areas outside of asia for example we  have um faculty who teach american  politics um and that that is appealing  part because um we we try to um promote  international higher education and our  students are very interested in  on knowledges something like you know  even  judaism or even you know um  european history so so in a way it's not  a disadvantage if you study something on  that is not based in asia  maybe i can  follow the the same order um  as the previous question so just for the  part of the uk to be honest i think my  advice on this question  um would be to just pay very close  attention to the department or faculty  that is advertising the job which i  realize is quite general advice but i  think  it's particularly applicable in a case  like cambridge or other uk universities  where the institutional configurations  can be very unique so for example at  cambridge  the history faculty up until recently  had one person working on southeast asia  who uh connected to various aspects of  overseas chinese communities  um  rachel leo whose work i'm sure i'm  underselling in all kinds of ways  but  most of the teaching in  what we would consider asian history was  happening in the faculty of asian and  middle eastern studies so a totally  different faculty and i think possibly  even a separate school within the  university um and there wasn't any kind  of in my position was created de novo  new  um in history and philosophy of science  so  um it really i think is probably just a  lesson to think carefully about the  department that's advertising the  position  and how your work would fit into the  existing faculties  research interests and themes  really as much as anything so  thank you we actually have two questions  about where you find job announcements  outside of the us so i wonder if we  could go around and just um in a quick  list uh name the top sites where you  found postings um about either your  positions or international positions  so i think we all check the each with  each net i i've been away from the job  market for a while so i'm not familiar  right h nat um and um but i i agree like  some of the universities outside the us  are not very close to someone's posting  all their jobs um on these platforms so  i'd be happy to stand the links to the  two positions i talked about  at my home institution and another thing  i recently thought very useful is  academic twitter so lots of job posts i  i got notified on twitter um in person  yes um i i  i i did this same thing i i i look at  jobs um at h-net and uh  uh i also joined a lot of many lists as  well as looking through this uh job  posting at different kind of uh um  associations website  um also i i just use google a lot  actually there's a lot of jobs  if you just search your field  and then tenure check on google and that  would pop up a lot of information so so  i look at that as well  so there's one website for uk academic  jobs where i think all of them are  posted which is jobs.ac.uk  and that gives you a smorgasbord of  different options and you can filter it  as as needed but that's what i would  normally direct  people to uh for looking at uk positions  thank you so much for this i see that  we're almost out of time  i was thinking that perhaps we could ask  uh one teaching and one research  question that um  any one of you can respond to if you  like it doesn't have to be everyone um  but uh we have a question from joshua  tan what are some of the challenges and  opportunities regarding teaching or  course design for non-us contexts  in other words  are the  the  syllabi that you've designed in the u.s  are they relevant and how did you adapt  yours  i think being in asia allow you to teach  more niche topics because the students  have  they really have a good background  knowledge about a region in their  pre-universal clinical education for  example i teach topics such as modern  indonesia world war ii in south east  asia these probably probably are not  really pragmatic in the western context  but um in terms of the general  curriculum such as introduction to asia  pacific i found it really interesting to  reframe some of the debates and topics  in the asia  context in the sense that you don't  you know what we teach what research are  usually framed by the political  discourse  or the larger political context where  we're situated so it's it's kind of fun  to to rework on these frameworks and  reshape the for example discussion  question or debate topics but i think  the general like in the question like  what you  read or work on during  during your qualification exam that's  still very useful um but  you probably need to frame it in a  different way  thank you does anybody else want to  chime in on teaching  i was just going to briefly say that  teaching at  cambridge and more generally in the  oxbridge system is so weird and unusual  that i had to learn a whole new  vocabulary  where you know students aren't in years  they're in part two or part three  um and i think for me the biggest change  was giving many fewer lectures  and therefore having to squeeze and  compress a lot more material very  quickly  especially because the very specialized  nature of the uk educational system  means that  students only study a few topics in  depth before going to university and so  they have a lot of really intense  knowledge about several topics but not  necessarily  a breadth of knowledge not necessarily  very much knowledge at all about um asia  or asian history in particular so it can  be that can be a challenge but also  really fascinating and rewarding in  different ways  thank you so much  i think for me it's um  because china and the u.s are so  different so sometimes my students do  not know certain things that have that  have been censored by the chinese  government so in my class i would i  would actually teach them you know um i  would i would mention things like chem  and square event and  um among other things and i would  encourage my students to use google  in  instead  instead of baidu if you can imagine that  they're in channel um other than that  actually i've found that students are  really interesting once they started to  learn this kind of stuff they feel very  i guess inspired and they tend to ask me  a lot of questions about you know things  that we taught in you know either in  chinese history or your or you know you  know western philosophy something like  that and uh that part is uh it's a great  rewarding experience for me  thank you so much for sharing those  experiences let's ask a final research  question that i will tag on a couple of  the individual questions um so an  anonymous attendee asks about um human  subjects research infrastructure  protocol in your institution for example  if you use ethnographic methods um so  that's a question director for the whole  panel  i  i i think doing research in china is  great because in a way um you you can do  your field work all the time by just  talking to people and trying different  kind of things um  on the uh recent crackdown on academia  does not really affect us much um  frankly um  when we designed dku i i learned that we  we  we pretty much want to protect academic  freedom at dku meaning that you teach  whatever you like to teach  and uh in the end dq will defend you if  um if that is saying as problematic by  the chinese government but but of course  uh we are speaking outside of  on you will have to take a personal risk  in terms of what you you say and what  you do um but but  my experience is that um i do research  on religion and uh and for example  earlier this year i visited koshkar and  uh 